  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 404 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nAs the long term period begins on Thursday night, Georgia will \nremain positioned between a high pressure over the Mid-Atlantic and \nlow pressure over the northeastern Gulf. The position and strength \nof the high will allow for a CAD wedge along the lee side of the \nAppalachian and into north Georgia, with relatively drier, more \nstable air underneath. Meanwhile, moisture will continue to spread \ninto Georgia on the northeastern side of the low. This advection of \nwarm and moist air will contribute to dewpoints and low temperatures \nin the low to mid 50s in areas south of I-20 by early Friday \nmorning, compared to 40s in areas to the north. Precipitable water \nvalues will climb to between 1 inch in far north Georgia to 1.6 \ninches in south-central Georgia. It should also be noted that a \ntight pressure gradient between the two aforementioned features will \ndrive breezy easterly winds across the forecast area through \nSaturday.\n\nAs the low meanders towards the Southeast, scattered showers are \nexpected in central Georgia on Friday. With warmest temperatures and \nthe highest humidity in portions of east-central Georgia, an \nisolated thunderstorm also cannot be ruled out. Uncertainty still\nremains with respect to the coverage of precipitation and how far\nnorth it will spread. The northward extent of deeper moisture and\nprecipitation will be determined in large part by the evolution \nand track of the low and also by the position and strength of the \nwedge. On Saturday, a more robust low is progged to develop along \nthe Florida peninsula and move northward towards the Carolina \ncoast. This will focus rain chances in the eastern parts of the \nforecast area, though more widespread heavy rain is currently \nexpected to stay east of Georgia. Drier air on the back side of \nthis low and a mid-level ridge traversing the Southeast is \nanticipated to promote benign weather on Sunday and Monday.\n\nLow level moisture and increased cloud cover will contribute to low \ntemperatures in the upper 40s through mid 50s each morning over the \nweekend and into the early parts of next week. These lows will be \nbetween 10-15 degrees above daily normals. High temperatures will \nlargely be in the upper 60s and low 70s each afternoon.",
